                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_vrin_right:    `<strong>That is the right asset.</strong> The sustainability and impact specialism is the one thing scoring above 70 — valuable to target employers, rare in your market, and genuinely hard to imitate because it took years of faculty and partners to build. Everything else is a commodity. Concentrate there.`,
                    coach_r1_rankings:      `<strong>Rankings are an output, not a capability.</strong> You cannot win the league tables Concorde now out-resources, and a ranking is not Valuable-Rare-Inimitable in itself. What underlying capability would move a ranking that a rival cannot quickly buy?`,
                    coach_r1_price:         `<strong>Price is the opposite of a capability.</strong> Being cheaper is the easiest thing for a €20M rival to match — and it is exactly the spending war you must avoid. A low price is neither rare nor inimitable; it just thins your 8% margin.`,
                    coach_r1_breadth:       `<strong>Breadth is your problem, not your asset.</strong> Nine me-too programs is precisely the stuck-in-the-middle position the board wants fixed. 'Something for everyone' is distinctive in nothing. Which single capability is actually rare and hard to copy?`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named the inimitable capability AND read Concorde's real weakness — it bought a campaign, but it has no built faculty depth or impact centre. That gap is exactly where a position it cannot quickly copy can be built. Now concentrate there.`,
                    coach_r1_brand:         `<strong>You will not out-market a €20M global campaign.</strong> Concorde's brand spend dwarfs yours. Its weakness is not marketing — it is the absence of the deep, earned capability you already hold. Compete on what it cannot buy quickly.`,
                    coach_r1_ai:            `<strong>Launching a deeper AI master plays to Concorde's chosen ground.</strong> You have no AI faculty depth or track record; it does. Distinctiveness has to be defensible — anchor it in the capability that is genuinely yours, not in matching its label.`,
                    coach_r1_money:         `<strong>'Strong everywhere' is the analysis that leads to surrender.</strong> A €20M war-chest buys reach, but it cannot instantly buy a built research centre, deep faculty and earned partner trust. Find the gap money cannot quickly close — that is where you compete.`,
                    coach_r1_lowhonesty:    `<strong>This is how the stuck-in-the-middle position survives another cycle.</strong> Low willingness to make trade-offs means you will add a program rather than stop one, and dilute further. Name the problem honestly: you must stop being everything to everyone.`,
                    coach_r2_concentrate:   `<strong>Concentration is the discipline that creates distinctiveness.</strong> Funding three programs anchored in your real capability past the differentiation threshold beats nine half-funded me-toos. This is how a smaller school out-positions a richer one — focus, not spread.`,
                    coach_r2_four:          `<strong>Partial focus is better than spread, but it still hedges.</strong> Four or five lightly-funded programs may not all cross the threshold. The benchmark distinctive schools concentrate hard. How much sharper would the position be on three?`,
                    coach_r2_spread:        `<strong>You funded all nine evenly — fair to everyone, distinctive in nothing.</strong> €667k each builds no program past the threshold. This is peanut-butter allocation: the classic error. Which programs are you willing to stop so the ones that matter can actually become distinctive?`,
                    coach_r2_rivalcopy:     `<strong>An 'AI & quantum-finance' master fights Concorde on its ground with assets you lack.</strong> You concentrated — but on a position you cannot defend. Concentration only works when it is on a capability that is genuinely, inimitably yours.`,
                    coach_r2_nostop:        `<strong>You kept all nine programs.</strong> Without stopping anything you cannot concentrate resources past the threshold — you have simply preserved the stuck-in-the-middle portfolio the board asked you to fix. The power of strategy is as much in the 'no' as the 'yes'.`,
                    coach_r2_overcut:       `<strong>You have cut too deep.</strong> Discontinuing almost everything destroys the revenue base, the faculty and the school's breadth of accreditation — you cannot rebuild distinctiveness from rubble. Concentrate on 3–4, do not dismantle the institution.`,
                    coach_r2_war:           `<strong>You have started a price war you cannot win.</strong> You put serious money into matching Concorde's discount — against a rival with three times your war-chest — and thinned your already-fragile margin. What do you have that money cannot quickly buy, and why are you not competing there instead?`,
                    coach_r3_owned:         `<strong>This is a defensible position.</strong> The impact-leadership school is built on your research centre, corporate guarantees and faculty — exactly the assets Concorde bought its way around but did not build. It cannot quickly copy this, which is what makes it defensible.`,
                    coach_r3_match:         `<strong>Matching feature-for-feature and undercutting tuition is the losing game.</strong> You are fighting a €20M rival on price and sameness — the very trap. Undercutting also pushes your 8% margin toward deficit. Differentiate on what is yours; do not mirror what is theirs.`,
                    coach_r3_quantum:       `<strong>That position is differentiated — and undeliverable.</strong> You have no AI or quantum-finance faculty, partners or track record, while Concorde does. Distinctiveness has to be defensible: which of your real capabilities supports the claim you are making?`,
                    coach_r3_generalist:    `<strong>A solid generalist MBA is the definition of stuck-in-the-middle.</strong> 'Safe and broadly appealing' is what a dozen rivals already offer. It does not differentiate and it does not defend against Concorde. Anchor the flagship in the capability that is uniquely yours.`,
                    coach_r3_defensible:    `<strong>These are differentiators a rival cannot easily copy.</strong> A corporate-partner impact guarantee, a research-led curriculum and named-faculty outcomes all rest on assets Concorde lacks. That is differentiation built on capability, not on a discount it can instantly match.`,
                    coach_r3_mixed:         `<strong>A discount and a new logo is not differentiation.</strong> A glossy brochure does not change what the program delivers, and the discount only feeds the price war. Concorde can match both in a week. Build differentiators rooted in what only you can deliver.`,
                    coach_r3_aimodule:      `<strong>Bolting 'AI' onto everything chases Concorde's label.</strong> A thin module on every program signals me-too modernity, not distinctiveness, and stretches you onto its ground. Depth in your own capability beats a shallow imitation of theirs.`,
                    coach_r3_retain:        `<strong>You treated the professor as the core of the strategy, not a cost line.</strong> A research-chair package retains the person in whom the inimitable capability actually lives. Without her there is no defensible flagship — retaining her IS the strategy.`,
                    coach_r3_partial:       `<strong>A modest raise against a €20M rival's offer is a coin-flip.</strong> If she leaves, the capability your whole position rests on walks to Concorde and your flagship loses its defensibility. Capabilities live in people — under-investing here risks everything else you built.`,
                    coach_r3_letgo:         `<strong>You let the capability walk to your rival.</strong> 'The strategy is bigger than one person' is wrong when the strategy rests on a capability that lives in that person. With her gone to Concorde, what exactly is left that is inimitable? You have hollowed out your own flagship.`,
                    coach_r4_premium:       `<strong>A defensible premium plus the freed cost makes it viable.</strong> Differentiation earns the right to a premium price, and discontinuing the me-too programs frees the cost to fund the flagship. This is how distinctiveness pays for itself within the cycle — exactly what the board asked for.`,
                    coach_r4_volume:        `<strong>Chasing volume with deep discounts undoes the differentiation.</strong> Deep discounts to fill seats erode the premium your distinctiveness earns and reignite the price war. A distinctive offer is not a discounted offer.`,
                    coach_r4_subsidy:       `<strong>Running at a loss and hoping rankings fix it fails the viability test.</strong> A flagship that loses money pushes the 8% school into deficit and triggers the very cuts you were trying to avoid. Distinctiveness must be reconciled with margin now, not later.`,
                    coach_r4_sequenced:     `<strong>A sequenced roadmap turns the plan into execution.</strong> Launch the flagship, protect the faculty, and evidence outcomes for AACSB and the rankings in order — that respects the 18-month horizon and the re-accreditation stakes on program cuts.`,
                    coach_r4_cutfast:       `<strong>Cutting fast and ignoring governance is reckless.</strong> Discontinuing programs without faculty-governance and accreditation sequencing risks the AACSB visit and a faculty revolt — both of which can sink the repositioning. Pace the cuts within the 18-month plan.`,
                    coach_r4_waitsee:       `<strong>Waiting hands the initiative to Concorde.</strong> Its scholarships are poaching your admitted students now, and the board gave you ten weeks. 'Wait and see' is how the 12% application drop becomes real. Commit to the distinctive plan and move.`,
                    coach_r4_evidence:      `<strong>That is a board-ready case.</strong> You linked capability to position, showed the margin bridge, and justified what you stopped and why. A distinctive offer that is also defensible and viable — that is a repositioning the board can back against a richer rival.`,
                    coach_r4_spend:         `<strong>Promising to keep spending until you win is the unwinnable war restated.</strong> You have three times less war-chest than Concorde; matching it to the finish line bankrupts the margin. The board asked for a defensible AND viable position, not a spending pledge.`,
                    coach_r4_hope:          `<strong>'The finances will follow' is not a financial plan.</strong> Distinctiveness without a margin bridge is exactly the flagship that quietly pushes the school into deficit. Show how it pays for itself this cycle, or the board will not — and should not — back it.`,

